So, um, what happens when an officer in your ministry faces a list of twenty values?

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,120
Well, ask an officer to pick one value from a list of twenty, and she scrolls, reads, and, uh, sometimes picks the neighbor Right

6
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:28,660
But the method has a plain rule for this, you see.

7
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:31,780
And it costs nothing to apply while the application is designed

8
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,740
Okay, so what is the specific rule for these list lengths?

9
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:42,680
No step of a choice offers more than nine values, and, uh, seven is really the aim Seven, okay Yeah.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,540
A longer list is divided into categories, each holding no more than nine values

11
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:49,500
So the officer picks the category first

12
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,060
Exactly, the category first, then the value.

13
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,000
And if a list ever needs more than nine categories, well, another level is added above them

14
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:57,980
Got it.

15
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,340
And what about the categories themselves?

16
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:02,960
The categories are just a list like any other.

17
